// F46 — a function as a value (ticket 75): the arrow `fn(T) -> U` is a type,
// a lambda `(a, b) => e` is an expression, a name in value position is that
// function, and a bound name is called like any other.
//
//   bsc --src-root examples examples/Shop/Pricing/Pricing.bs Charged :member 250
//   bsc --src-root examples examples/Shop/Pricing/Pricing.bs Owed "[(:a, 3), (:b, 4)]"
//   bsc --src-root examples examples/Shop/Pricing/Pricing.bs Doubled "[1, 2, 3]"
//
// Three forms, one type: `Rule` returns an arrow as a lambda in two clauses
// and as a private function's name in the third, and `Charge` calls whichever
// arrived through the parameter it was bound to.
module Shop.Pricing

// An arrow in a signature's return position, and a lambda as a clause body.
// A lambda has one clause and one expression for a body; its parameters are
// patterns, irrefutable against the arrow's domain.
public fn(int) -> int Rule(atom tier)
Rule(:standard) -> (cents) => cents
Rule(:member)   -> (cents) => cents - 100
Rule(:staff)    -> Free
Rule(_)         -> (cents) => cents

// An arrow in parameter position, and a call through the bound name.
public int Charge(fn(int) -> int rule, int cents)
Charge(rule, cents) -> rule(cents)

public int Charged(atom tier, int cents)
Charged(tier, cents) -> Charge(Rule(tier), cents)

// A name in value position: `Free` reads its arity from the arrow `Rule`
// declares. Where nothing fixes it, `Free/1` is the spelling.
private int Free(int cents)
Free(_) -> 0

// The function-taking operations under `List` take the fun as an argument,
// one walker per module per operation, as `List.Sum` is emitted. A lambda's
// parameter is a pattern: `(_, n)` takes the pair apart in the head.
public int Owed(list<(atom, int)> pairs)
Owed(pairs) -> pairs |> List.Fold(0, (acc, (_, n)) => acc + n)

public list<int> Doubled(list<int> xs)
Doubled(xs) -> List.Map(xs, Double/1)

public list<int> Large(list<int> xs)
Large(xs) -> xs |> List.Filter(n => n > 100)

private int Double(int n)
Double(n) -> n * 2
